# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Wayfinder environment — ADVANCED / OPTIONAL OVERRIDES
#
# You do NOT need to edit this file for a normal first run. The zero-env path is:
#
#     docker compose up -d      # Postgres + MinIO
#     ./restart.sh              # generates secrets, migrates, starts the app
#     # → open the printed  http://localhost:3000/setup?token=…  link
#     # → create the admin, then configure everything in the setup wizard
#
# restart.sh auto-generates the three secrets (SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY,
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, SCHEDULER_TICK_SECRET) and DATABASE_URL ships a working
# default. Every integration below (AI, storage, mail, n8n, Entra) is an OPTIONAL
# override — the setup wizard configures each in-app and stores it in the
# database. Set a var here only if you want to preset it or run without the
# wizard.
#
# Every value below may be left blank: a blank line is read as "not set", so the
# schema default applies. Uncommenting a var and leaving it empty is safe.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# App
NODE_ENV=development

# Ports. WEB_PORT also has to agree with BETTER_AUTH_URL below — Better Auth
# signs and validates callbacks against that origin.
WEB_PORT=3000
API_PORT=3001

# Database (Postgres + pgvector)
# Default matches the docker-compose Postgres, which publishes container port
# 5432 on host port 5433 — so the host-side app connects on 5433.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/wayfinder

# Whether the web app applies pending migrations before it starts.
#
# Default is `true`, which is what makes `./restart.sh` work from a clean
# checkout with no database step. DEPLOYMENTS SHOULD SET THIS TO `false` and run
# migrations as their own step instead — `docker run … migrate`, an ECS one-off
# task, a Container Apps job — so that a rolling deploy does not start several
# instances that all migrate at once, and so a migration failure is a failed
# step rather than a crash-looping web container. See ADR-047.
RUN_MIGRATIONS_ON_START=true
# Where the generated SQL migrations live. Only needed when the usual relative
# lookup cannot work — the container image sets it, because the API bundle's
# location tells it nothing about where the SQL was copied to.
MIGRATIONS_DIR=

# Auth
# AUTH_METHOD options: email-password | pki | pki-and-email-password | google-oauth | other
AUTH_METHOD=email-password
# Signs Better Auth sessions. Required on startup — restart.sh auto-generates one
# into .env if this is blank, so you rarely set it by hand.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# The origin the app is served on. Sign-in callbacks, notification email links
# and the first-run /setup link are all built from it, so a deployment behind a
# real hostname MUST set this — the localhost default will not work there.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Encrypts secret-bearing system settings (AI/storage/n8n/auth/email configs)
# at rest with AES-256-GCM. Required on startup. restart.sh auto-generates one
# into .env if blank (or generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`).
SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# OPTIONAL fallback. Leave blank to type the admin email on the first-run /setup
# screen. When set, the setup screen pre-fills it AND binds bootstrap to it (only
# this email may create the admin).
ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=
# OPTIONAL. Override the one-time first-run setup token (for automated installs).
# Leave blank to let the app generate and persist one, printed to the startup log.
SETUP_TOKEN=

# Microsoft Entra ID sign-in (ADR-025). Optional fallbacks — the admin-settings
# Authentication card overrides these when an auth_config row exists. When all
# three are set and no DB row overrides them, Entra sign-in is enabled at boot.
# In the Azure app registration, add this redirect URI under the Web platform:
#   ${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/microsoft
ENTRA_TENANT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Overrides the identity-provider host (default https://login.microsoftonline.com).
# Set this for a sovereign cloud, or point it at the local mock Entra server:
#   ./restart.sh --with-mocks   →   http://localhost:4001/entra
ENTRA_AUTHORITY=

# PKI / client-certificate auth (required when AUTH_METHOD includes "pki")
# Comma-separated IPs of reverse proxies trusted to forward cert headers.
# MUST be set and non-empty when PKI auth is enabled — leave blank for email/password.
# To drive this locally against the mock mTLS proxy, use ./restart.sh --with-pki,
# which sets AUTH_METHOD=pki-and-email-password and PKI_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=127.0.0.1
# for you, then present a certificate at http://localhost:4001/pki/connect
PKI_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=
PKI_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=8

# AI Providers
# AI_DEFAULT_PROVIDER options: anthropic | openai | mistral | bedrock
AI_DEFAULT_PROVIDER=anthropic
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# Amazon Bedrock (used when AI_DEFAULT_PROVIDER=bedrock).
# All three values must be set together — leaving any blank falls back to whatever
# is configured in the admin Settings page (or, if nothing is stored there, no creds).
AWS_BEDROCK_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_BEDROCK_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_BEDROCK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Object Storage (MinIO / S3-compatible)
# Read by both apps. For local development with docker-compose, the defaults
# below work out of the box. For Railway with an external S3-compatible store:
# point MINIO_ENDPOINT at your provider.
#
# For production on Amazon S3, all four of these matter:
#   MINIO_ENDPOINT=s3.<region>.amazonaws.com
#   MINIO_USE_SSL=true
#   MINIO_REGION=<region>      S3 signs with the bucket's region. Left blank, the
#                              client spends a GetBucketLocation call per bucket to
#                              discover it, and fails outright if the credentials
#                              are not granted s3:GetBucketLocation.
#   MINIO_PATH_STYLE=false     S3 deprecated path-style addressing; newer buckets
#                              require virtual-hosted style. MinIO needs it true.
MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost
MINIO_PORT=9000
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_BUCKET=wayfinder-documents
MINIO_USE_SSL=false
MINIO_REGION=
MINIO_PATH_STYLE=true

# Observability (Langfuse - stubbed unless both keys are present)
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
LANGFUSE_HOST=http://localhost:3030

# OpenTelemetry (apps/api only — traces emitted only when the endpoint is set)
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=wayfinder-api

# Scheduler heartbeat (ADR-019) — the poller in apps/api that fires due
# scheduled sessions by POSTing the web app's internal tick endpoint. On by
# default, but it cannot start until SCHEDULER_TICK_SECRET is set, because the
# endpoint refuses an unauthenticated tick; restart.sh generates one into .env.
# The API and the web app must see the SAME value. With it unset, the API logs
# "Scheduler enabled but not started" and scheduled sessions never fire.
SCHEDULER_TICK_SECRET=
# Defaults to ${WEB_BASE_URL}/api/internal/scheduler/tick. Set it only when the
# API reaches the web app on a different address (split deploys, internal DNS).
SCHEDULER_TICK_URL=
# Base URL apps/api uses for the web app: the tick endpoint above and the links
# in notification emails. The web app uses BETTER_AUTH_URL for the same job.
WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Set SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false to opt out of scheduled sessions entirely.
SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true

# Extraction batch engine (ADR-033 §6) — the poller in apps/api that advances
# "Synthesise Information" runs. On by default; set it to false to opt out.
# With it off, a run only progresses while its run screen is open in a browser,
# so a long batch left unattended stalls. Intake limits and the per-run spend
# ceiling are admin settings, not env vars.
EXTRACTION_WORKER_ENABLED=true
EXTRACTION_TICK_MS=5000

# n8n callback webhook (apps/api). Shared secret the n8n workflow signs its
# callback with; the webhook route rejects every call while this is unset.
N8N_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Email notifications (ADR-023)
# NOTIFICATIONS_ENABLED=false still writes outbox rows to app_notification_log
# but skips the send. It is an env-only switch with no admin override, so it has
# to be true here for any mail to leave the app.
#
# SMTP_TRANSPORT_MODE is deliberately BLANK by default: setting it makes the
# environment the source of truth for mail and SILENTLY OVERRIDES whatever an
# admin configures in Configuration → Email. Leave it blank to use the in-app
# settings. Options, when you do want env-only mail:
#   stream — local sink for dev/tests: messages are built but never delivered.
#   smtp   — generic relay with SMTP AUTH (requires SMTP_HOST/SMTP_USER/SMTP_PASS).
#   oauth2 — Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online via XOAUTH2 (requires the M365_* vars;
#            host defaults to smtp.office365.com:587).
# Credentials here are environment-only — never stored in the DB.
NOTIFICATIONS_ENABLED=false
SMTP_TRANSPORT_MODE=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=wayfinder@example.com
M365_TENANT_ID=
M365_CLIENT_ID=
M365_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Overrides the Microsoft Graph hosts the directory adapters call — the API base
# (default https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0) and the token authority (default
# https://login.microsoftonline.com). Set them for a sovereign cloud, or point
# them at the local mock Graph, which serves the same 100-person roster the mock
# HR spreadsheet does:
#   ./restart.sh --with-mocks   →   http://localhost:4001/graph/v1.0
#                                   http://localhost:4001/entra
# Both are inert on their own: Graph is only consulted once all three M365
# credentials above are set.
M365_GRAPH_BASE_URL=
M365_AUTHORITY=
